<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Woyzeck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1836">1836</a>–<a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Woyzeck is an unfinished play by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-buchner">Georg Büchner</a>, written in 1836-1837 and left in fragments at his death. It tells of a poor soldier, exploited and humiliated, who murders the woman he loves. Published in 1879 and first staged in 1913, it is regarded as a forerunner of modern drama and inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-berg">Alban Berg</a>'s opera Wozzeck.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The play is loosely based on the true story of Johann Christian Woyzeck, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> wigmaker who later became a soldier. In 1821, in a fit of jealousy, he murdered Christiane Woost, a 46-year-old widow with whom he had been living, and he was later publicly beheaded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-buchner">Georg Büchner</a> left his play in a fragmentary state when he died in February 1837, and it was first made public only through a heavily edited and augmented version by Karl Emil Franzos, who published it in periodicals and then in his edition of Büchner's collected works.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Franz Woyzeck is a lonely soldier stationed in a provincial German town, living with Marie, the mother of his child, who was born out of wedlock. He earns extra money by doing menial jobs for the Captain and by taking part in medical experiments conducted by the Doctor, who tells him that he must eat nothing but peas. His mental health breaks down, and he begins to experience apocalyptic visions. Marie turns her attention to a handsome drum major.</p><p>Woyzeck confronts the drum major, who beats and humiliates him, and then stabs Marie to death by a pond. The fragment as Büchner left it ends with Woyzeck disposing of the knife in the pond while trying to clean himself of the blood. Some scholars, notably A. H. J. Knight and Fritz Bergemann, hold that a trial scene was part of the original conception, and what may be the start of a courtroom scene survives. Franzos inserted the stage direction &quot;ertrinkt&quot; (he drowns), an emendation that Knight said almost amounts to a forgery, though most versions still use drowning as the ending.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Franzos struggled with Büchner's microscopically small handwriting, and the pages had faded so badly that they had to be chemically treated to make the text readable. He misread the title character's name in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> as Wozzeck, and the play bore that title in its first productions. It was first performed at the Residenztheater in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 8 November 1913, produced by Max Reinhardt. A new edition by Georg Witkowski in 1921 introduced the corrected title Woyzeck.</p><p>Woyzeck has become one of the most influential and most often performed German plays. Because it is unfinished, productions have taken liberties with the order of scenes, and each arrangement makes a different statement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-berg">Alban Berg</a> made an opera from it in 1925. Ingmar Bergman directed a stage version at the Royal Dramatic Theatre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> in 1969, and Werner Herzog directed a film in 1979. Later productions reset the story in a psychiatric hospital, in an army of 1951 and in a distribution centre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Woyzeck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Georg Büchner, born near Darmstadt, wrote Danton's Death and <b>Woyzeck</b> before his early death in exile in 1837.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
